						<Item class="StringValue" referent="427">
							<Properties>
								<string name="Value"><![CDATA[# Composition.adaptation

Top-bar strip layout, fallback and overlays.

- A region with `zone = "topbar"` goes into the free strip of the Roblox top
  bar, level with the Roblox buttons. The composition puts these regions in a
  second ScreenGui with `ScreenInsets = TopbarSafeInsets`, in the parent of its
  own ScreenGui. That ScreenGui has the same `DisplayOrder`, follows the
  `Enabled` of the host and links the same StyleSheet. The regions sit side
  by side. Leading regions sit beside the Roblox buttons. Trailing regions
  (`trailing = true`) sit at the far end of the strip, such as a menu button.
  The other regions stay centred in the remaining space.
  Regions step down when their total width with the gaps does not fit the strip,
  or when a region is taller than the strip row.
- When `topbar` is `false`, when `GuiService.TopbarInset` has no width (a
  console has no Roblox top bar), or when the composition is not in a
  ScreenGui, these regions come first in the `top` zone. A trailing region
  goes first in the `topRight` zone instead, so a menu button keeps its corner.
- When the strip cannot hold a region at its last form, that region moves to
  the start of the `top` zone in its richest form. The highest rank moves first.
  With equal ranks, the later region moves first. The other regions stay in
  the strip. Thus a long row of chips on a portrait phone moves below the bar
  and does not cover the Roblox buttons.
- The strip ScreenGui stays in place while empty. The composition can measure
  it and return regions when the strip becomes wider, for example after a rotation.
- The strip row aligns with the Roblox buttons. It is 44 pixels high and sits
  2 pixels above the bottom of the band. Regions centre on the Roblox buttons.
  A region taller than 44 pixels does not fit the strip.
  The leading row starts one `gap` after the Roblox buttons.
  The strip keeps one `gap` from the screen edge.
  Give a button beside the Roblox buttons a 44 pixel height.
  A `compact` circle in the neutral theme has this height.
- A Popover, Menu or other overlay from a strip region shows in the composition's
  ScreenGui. Thus the narrow strip does not clip it.
- In the strip, the `Topbar` Frame holds a `Leading` row for leading regions.
  Its `Middle` row centres the other regions, and its `Trailing` row holds the
  trailing regions at the end.
  In the `top` zone, a `Landed` Frame holds the regions that moved; in the
  `topRight` zone, `LandedTrailing` holds the trailing ones.
]]></string>
								<BinaryString name="AttributesSerialize"></BinaryString>
								<SecurityCapabilities name="Capabilities">0</SecurityCapabilities>
								<bool name="DefinesCapabilities">false</bool>
								<UniqueId name="HistoryId">00000000000000000000000000000000</UniqueId>
								<string name="Name">Composition.adaptation.md</string>
								<BinaryString name="SerializedOverrides"></BinaryString>
								<int64 name="SourceAssetId">-1</int64>
								<SharedString name="Tags">yuZpQdnvvUBOTYh1jqZ2cA==</SharedString>
								<UniqueId name="UniqueId">1f5f3983a820f33d0aedc38900000587</UniqueId>
							</Properties>
						</Item>
						<Item class="StringValue" referent="428">
							<Properties>
								<string name="Value"><![CDATA[# Composition.call

Put presented Sheet and Dialog controls beside Composition in Screen. A
Region measures HUD content and does not define a modal viewport.

`UI.Composition(spec) -> Frame` places ranked regions at the edges and
corners of the screen, as a game HUD does. `UI.Region(spec) -> Frame` is one
of those regions. Put the composition in a ScreenGui, or in any Frame that
covers the area to use. It fills its parent. The ScreenGui `ScreenInsets`
keeps it inside the device safe area. When the composition reaches into the
`GuiService.TopbarInset` band, it adds the covered height to its top padding,
as `Screen` does.

A composition is a HUD over live gameplay, so it is a passive
[responder](responder.md): a stick push or a D-pad press never selects a HUD
control, Space reaches the game, and a click does not take the keyboard. Open
HUD tasks for a gamepad with game actions, or set `GuiService.SelectedObject`
to a HUD control from a game action.

```luau
local hidden = Compose.cell(1)
return UI.Composition "Hud" {
	UI.Region "Score" { zone = "top", rank = 1, UI.Text { text = "12 : 9" } },
	UI.Region "Tasks" {
		zone = "left",
		rank = 3,
		mayDrop = true,
		form = hidden,
		UI.Text { text = "Win a round, land 25 hits" },
		UI.Button { label = "Tasks 1/2" },
	},
	UI.Region "Objective" { zone = "topbar", rank = 2, UI.Text { text = "Round 3" } },
}
```

Composition options:

- `gap`: the space between the regions of one zone, between the three lanes
  and between the zones of one lane. The default is `s`.
- `padding`: the space inside the edges. The default is `s`.
- `topbar`: a boolean or a readable. The default is `true`.
  See [Composition.adaptation](Composition.adaptation.md) for top-bar behavior.
- `rows`: `"match"` or `"fit"`. The default is `"match"`: the regions that
  share a row of the `top` or `bottom` zone take the height of the tallest one,
  so a set of HUD readouts reads as one row. `"fit"` keeps each region at its
  own height. A region whose content has its own `UISizeConstraint` keeps its
  height.
- `passive`: a boolean. The default is `true`, a passive responder as above.
  `false` registers no responder, so the D-pad enters the composition like
  any screen. Use it for a dedicated screen laid out with regions, not for a
  HUD over play.
- The children are `UI.Region` nodes. A `UI.fill()` child is also allowed, so
  the composition can take the free height of a stack.

See [Composition.regions](Composition.regions.md) for region options.
]]></string>
