							<Item class="StringValue" referent="948">
								<Properties>
									<string name="Value"><![CDATA[# Adaptive manipulation

Part of [game UI design](../GAME_UI_DESIGN_GUIDE.md).

Define the operation first: move, copy, apply, reorder, resize or adjust.
Keep source identity, eligibility and accepted result common across inputs.
Direct manipulation helps when position or movement explains the task. When
the player only needs a destination or a precise value, a command or value
control can be easier. For example, dragging equipment onto a slot and choosing
"Equip to slot" can invoke the same model operation.

## Choose the route before the gesture

Give the player a way to discover the operation, select its target, inspect
the proposed result, commit and cancel. Preserve these stages with touch,
pointer, keyboard and gamepad; the physical gesture can differ. The
alternative must work without precise movement or a sustained hold. Do not
require the player to learn dragging before the command route appears.

Use the owning Facet behavior rather than adding a second drag system.
Collections provide `drag`, `drop` and `rowDrop`. Use a separate `dropTarget`
for a distinct subtarget. `draggable` uses native UIDragDetector recognition
and provides pickup and drop through native selection. Custom adjusters can
use `adjustable`; commands on selected targets can use `commandScope`.
Read their API nodes before implementation. Native selection, scrolling and
drag detection supply mechanisms; the game owns what a move means.

## Avoid competing gestures

Touch pickup must coexist with scrolling. `draggable` waits for a long press;
movement first allows scrolling. Use an explicit edit mode
or the documented `armOnTap` route when a hold is unsuitable. In that route,
a tap picks up, scrolling remains available, and a target tap drops. Explain
the active mode and its cancellation. Keep source and destination feedback
visible around the held finger. Pointer movement should track directly,
without easing behind the cursor.

Give gamepad an explicit pickup, destination, commit and cancel path or an
equivalent command. Collection `onActivate` can take precedence over pickup
outside Edit mode; adding `drag` alone does not make one activation do both.
Show whether the next activation opens, picks up or commits. A press that
became a drag must not also activate the source or a control behind it.

Inspection input belongs to its preview. A press that opens or buys an object
must not also zoom or rotate its camera. Use ZoomView for a spatial board and
its documented pan, zoom and fit commands. Keep global actions outside zoomed
content. Provide discrete commands for any required adjustment that would
otherwise need continuous analog input.

Keep spatial bounds and scale stable as content appears. Reveal an offscreen
selection only as needed; do not recenter on every selection. If input,
viewport or source availability changes during manipulation, retain a clear
candidate or cancel safely. Never infer a committed drop from that change.

## Validate the destination

Show source identity, candidate destination and the proposed operation before
commitment. Distinguish valid, rejected and pending targets beyond color.
Give a useful rejection reason near the target or in stable task feedback.
Keep cancel available without losing the source.

`accepts` checks eligibility during targeting and release; it must not mutate
records or spend resources. A rejected visible target blocks a drop unless
`passThrough` explicitly permits it. Do not silently pass a destructive action
to something behind it. World targets need a reachable `focusTarget` for
non-pointer placement.

For pending drops, call `info.complete(true)` before changing the presentation
model, and apply that change only if it returns true. Complete refusal with
`info.complete(false, reason)`. Canceled or late completion cannot commit the
same UI operation twice. Reconcile durable server results independently.

The built-in drag feedback uses Travel. Preserve a return destination where
needed instead of creating unrelated copies of the carried object. Preserve
the same accepted result and cancellation when motion is reduced.

Test valid, invalid, occupied and disappearing targets; scrolling and recycling;
cancel; pending acceptance and rejection; and the equivalent command route.
Change input and viewport during an operation. Verify that neither cancellation
nor a delayed reply moves the wrong item or activates the source.
]]></string>
									<BinaryString name="AttributesSerialize"></BinaryString>
									<SecurityCapabilities name="Capabilities">0</SecurityCapabilities>
									<bool name="DefinesCapabilities">false</bool>
									<UniqueId name="HistoryId">00000000000000000000000000000000</UniqueId>
									<string name="Name">ADAPTIVE_INTERACTION_AND_DIRECT_MANIPULATION.md</string>
									<BinaryString name="SerializedOverrides"></BinaryString>
									<int64 name="SourceAssetId">-1</int64>
									<SharedString name="Tags">yuZpQdnvvUBOTYh1jqZ2cA==</SharedString>
									<UniqueId name="UniqueId">1f5f3983a820f33d0aedc38900000790</UniqueId>
								</Properties>
							</Item>
							<Item class="StringValue" referent="949">
								<Properties>
									<string name="Value"><![CDATA[# Choices and input

Part of [game UI design](../GAME_UI_DESIGN_GUIDE.md).

## Expose the decision

Make the available choices, current value and effect understandable before
the player acts. Prefer recognition over recall: show named alternatives when
comparison matters. Choose by meaning, precision and commitment, then adapt
the presentation to the room and input.

| Decision | Control |
| --- | --- |
| Independent boolean | Toggle |
| One value from a known set, with search if needed | Picker |
| Searchable choice with permitted custom values | ComboBox |
| Text or a precise number | TextInput or NumberInput |
| A bounded range | Slider |
| Exact increments | Stepper |
| A discrete amount or rating | LevelPicker or Rating |
| Compact filter or removable selection | Chip |
| Color with inspection | ColorPicker |
| An instant or interval | DateTimePicker |

Tabs navigate; they do not edit a value. A Button performs a command. Shared
voting and personal settings have different authority and feedback needs.
Do not use a command that cycles through hidden values when the player needs
to compare or correct a choice.

Picker's automatic style uses the available room, labels and input. Set an
explicit style when the decision needs it: `radioGroup` keeps explanations
visible, and `cards` can show meaningful visual alternatives. Automatic does
not select `cards`. Use Picker's `query` for a searchable known set; ComboBox
requires separate accepted `value`, editable `text` and `acceptCustom` policy.
Use ColorPicker for a color space; a few named colors can use Picker.

## Make adjustment understandable

Keep the label, current value, units, limits and error together. Sliders need
readable values and digital steps for gamepad or precise adjustment. Color
choices need visible swatches and names. Displayed numbers and parsing must
agree on units and precision.

Use field `label`, `hint` and `errorText` to retain the explanation while the
player types. A placeholder is an example or format hint, not the only label.
Explain what a setting changes and show a useful preview where its effect is
otherwise unclear. Offer a clear way to restore a previous or default value.
Do not require text entry when visible choices or steps serve the task better.

Slider's `step` and `onCommit` support bounded adjustment and the end of a
gesture. Stepper supplies exact increments. NumberInput keeps editable `value`
and committed `numericValue` in separate cells; `stepButtons` provides visible
increment and decrement actions. Match `parse`, `format`, bounds and precision
to the value the game actually accepts.

## Separate editing from acceptance

Reversible settings can preview immediately. Coupled edits need one draft and
an explicit commitment. Never purchase on incidental blur. Distinguish pending
work from accepted state; keep rejected input available for correction.
Incoming accepted values must not silently overwrite an active draft.

Text input must yield to native editing and IME. Define submit, cancel and
validation behavior from the task. TextInput's `onCommit` can run for `submit`
or `focusLost`; it does not mean the server saved the value. Its `validate`
returns accepted text or nil to reject. CellEditor's validator instead returns
an error message or nil, and its model callback owns acceptance. Use its
external `draft` for record edits that must survive recycling and conflicts.

ColorPicker and DateTimePicker offer `draft = true` for Apply and Cancel.
Their `onChange` callbacks propose values; their commit callbacks follow the
control's documented rules. Do not infer one control's save or cancellation
contract from another. Keep a draft in the game's model when several fields
share one accepted result.

## Verify the effect

Test touch, pointer and gamepad adjustment, long localized labels, invalid and
empty values, cancellation, delayed acceptance and reopening. A displayed
control or a changed label does not prove its effect.

Also open the native keyboard in the shortest supported layout. The active
field, its error and the route to submit or cancel must remain usable. Check
an input change during an edit without losing the player's draft.

Read `Picker.selected`, `Picker.menu`, `TextInput.reference`,
`NumberInput.reference`, `Stepper-and-Slider.value` and `CellEditor.draft`
for exact field behavior. Read `Tasks.state` for game authority and
`Sheet.dismissal` when the edit can leave through several routes.
]]></string>
									<BinaryString name="AttributesSerialize"></BinaryString>
									<SecurityCapabilities name="Capabilities">0</SecurityCapabilities>
									<bool name="DefinesCapabilities">false</bool>
									<UniqueId name="HistoryId">00000000000000000000000000000000</UniqueId>
									<string name="Name">CHOICES_AND_INPUT.md</string>
									<BinaryString name="SerializedOverrides"></BinaryString>
									<int64 name="SourceAssetId">-1</int64>
									<SharedString name="Tags">yuZpQdnvvUBOTYh1jqZ2cA==</SharedString>
									<UniqueId name="UniqueId">1f5f3983a820f33d0aedc38900000791</UniqueId>
								</Properties>
							</Item>
							<Item class="StringValue" referent="950">
								<Properties>
									<string name="Value"><![CDATA[# Collections and actions

Part of [game UI design](../GAME_UI_DESIGN_GUIDE.md).

## Choose the representation

Start with how the player recognizes and compares the objects. Show the
attributes that change the decision, in a consistent order and with common
units. Keep identity, availability and cost visible before commitment.
An unavailable object may still help the player plan if its reason and next
step are clear. Do not make them remember a previous object's facts to compare
it with the current one.

Use VirtualGrid for visual recognition, VirtualList for descriptions, Table
for shared attributes and Outline for an actual hierarchy. Item count alone
does not decide whether selection, reordering and collection focus are needed.
Use stable keys and keep durable row state in the model, outside recycled rows.

For bounded repeated content without collection interaction, Compose.keyed
keeps children associated with their model keys. Use the virtual controls when
rows need collection selection, activation, editing, reordering or windowing.
Facet supplies that interaction policy through native scrolling and selection;
do not add another row focus or click system.

Bound the collection viewport. Fixed item sizes must fit real content; use
the supported measurement option for varying content. Artwork should identify
the object, not decorate a repeated text button. Keep useful identity and
comparison visible in compact forms.

Keep browsing quieter than the selected object's inspection area. Match camera
angle, lighting and scale across comparable objects; frame each silhouette with
room for its extremes. Use a different view when inspection needs detail that
the collection thumbnail cannot show. A decorative image repeated across every
item does not establish identity. Show the selected object's name and status
beside its action, and distinguish that selection from the equipped object.

A spatial map or skill tree needs its authored relationships to remain visible,
not an Outline merely because its data is hierarchical. ZoomView can pan and
zoom a stable content tree. SpatialGrid is for a bounded inventory board with
explicit cell footprints and placement rules; it is not a world-map layout.

## Separate intentions

Focus identifies the next input target. Selection identifies an object under
consideration. Activation opens or operates it. Equip, Buy or Delete commits
a specific consequence. Do not treat these as interchangeable.

Bind collection `selection` when the player must see a persistent chosen set.
Use `onSelectionChange` for selection policy and `onActivate(item, key)` for
opening detail or entering actions. When supplied, `onSelectionChange` owns
accepting the proposed map with `selection:set(nextMap)`. With selection
enabled, a mouse click selects and a double click activates; touch and gamepad
activate directly outside collection editing mode.
Make every route identify the same object before commitment. For example,
moving focus across upgrades can reveal facts without buying an upgrade.

The `Tasks.collection-detail` recipe uses the model's selected key set to
derive one inspected object. Supply loaded items and exactly one valid selected
key. A mouse click updates the visible detail. In compact room, the visible
Inspect selection command opens it; activation provides the other input routes.
Only the detail action commits. Keep the selected key outside the page so the
chosen object returns when the page opens again. The caller handles loading,
empty, no-match and failed-request states, and removal of the chosen object.

Expose frequent important actions directly. Use Menu for secondary commands,
SplitButton for a meaningful default with alternatives, and RadialMenu for
quick spatial choices. A radial still needs readable names, a clear cancel
route and its compact fallback.

Use Card's supported actionable boundary and browseTarget relationship.
Avoid nested competing click targets or a second activation system over a
collection row. Set `reveal = "always"` when a frequent action must be
discoverable without hover. This does not wire gamepad entry: use
`Card.browseTarget` and `controls.enterActions()` to enter the action group
from collection activation without running its primary action. Back returns
to the browse stop. Hover feedback must stay within reserved paint space.

## Edit without losing state

Search, filter and sort the model. Preserve stable selection, favorites and
drafts through recycling and reopening. Show hidden-selection scope for bulk
actions. Set `selectionFrom` to the authoritative records on each filtered
view that must keep hidden selections; otherwise the collection prunes
selection against its visible source. Keep manual reorder separate from a
contradictory sort.

Show the active filter and result state, with a direct route to clear it.
Distinguish an empty collection from no matches, loading and a failed request.
Choose a useful initial order for the decision. Avoid moving the focused row
continually as live values change; make any requested sort and its direction
clear. If an update removes the selected object, explain the change and move
focus to a useful surviving target without acting on its behalf.

Read a virtual row's `current` in property bindings and `current:peek()` when
an action starts. A stable key can receive a replacement record. Capturing
the first record can leave its price or eligibility stale. Requests must use
the game's current validation and accepted state.

Use CellEditor for a record edit and RowActions for contextual row commands.
Full-swipe actions need particular care: disable fullSwipe on consequential
edges when an accidental swipe must not commit. Context commands must identify
whether they affect one row or the selected set.

Drag and command routes share validation and accepted results. Preserve
rejected drafts and handle late replies after row removal. See
[manipulation](ADAPTIVE_INTERACTION_AND_DIRECT_MANIPULATION.md) only when needed.

Test the first and last visible rows, empty and filtered-empty states, long
labels, updates while selected, recycling, focus restoration and every required
action using touch and gamepad as well as pointer.

Read `Tasks.collection-detail`, `VirtualList-and-VirtualGrid.from`,
`VirtualList-and-VirtualGrid.selection` and `Card.browseTarget` for the
interaction contract. Read `CellEditor.draft` for retained edits and
`Tasks.purchase` for pending and accepted actions.
]]></string>
									<BinaryString name="AttributesSerialize"></BinaryString>
									<SecurityCapabilities name="Capabilities">0</SecurityCapabilities>
									<bool name="DefinesCapabilities">false</bool>
									<UniqueId name="HistoryId">00000000000000000000000000000000</UniqueId>
									<string name="Name">COLLECTIONS_AND_ACTIONS.md</string>
									<BinaryString name="SerializedOverrides"></BinaryString>
									<int64 name="SourceAssetId">-1</int64>
									<SharedString name="Tags">yuZpQdnvvUBOTYh1jqZ2cA==</SharedString>
									<UniqueId name="UniqueId">1f5f3983a820f33d0aedc38900000792</UniqueId>
								</Properties>
							</Item>
							<Item class="StringValue" referent="951">
								<Properties>
									<string name="Value"><![CDATA[# Game flows

Part of [game UI design](../GAME_UI_DESIGN_GUIDE.md). Choose patterns by player
intent; these are not fixed screen templates.

## Set the pace and information need

Start with what the player must notice, decide and do while the world continues.
Use stable readouts for frequent checks and contextual detail for occasional decisions.
A planning task can support comparison that would distract during active play.
Keep deliberate game uncertainty separate from unclear commands or missing feedback.

Choose screen-space or world-space presentation by use. A world marker can explain
location, but distance, occlusion and camera movement affect its readability.
Keep essential commands and status accessible when the world cue cannot be read.
Use `WorldMarker.card` for target guidance and Host.SurfaceGui or Host.BillboardGui
for world UI. `DiegeticScreen.reference` moves an existing app between the screen
and a framed world surface. Test input and viewing distance in each presentation.

## During play

A HUD answers what matters now: resources and thresholds, the active objective,
the relevant target, and available commands. Keep readouts stable as values
change. Aggregate routine gains; give urgent state stronger emphasis.
Reserve room for changing numbers so targets do not shift as digits change.
Test simultaneous alerts; routine rewards must yield to immediate hazards.
ProgressView, Timer, ShortcutHint and WorldMarker supply their named behaviors.
Use `Tasks.hud` and `Tasks.safe-layout` for composition and movement clearance.

Use explicit verbs and game vocabulary for destinations. Keep one visible
entry per destination in each form. A reduced HUD must still expose required
tasks. End notices when their context ends.

## Choosing and preparing

Preserve object identity across browsing, preview, comparison and commitment.
Show owned, compatible, selected and equipped states separately. An empty slot
keeps its name. Show the actual candidate and meaningful differences before
Equip; the next play session must use the accepted choice.

Search helps known names; filters help unfamiliar content. Keep their combined
effect visible. Distinguish no matches, no items and load failure. Clear and
Reset are different actions. Preserve query, selection and favorites on return.

For purchases, crafting or trade, keep the complete terms beside commitment.
A change to price, quantity or reviewed terms invalidates an old confirmation.
Pending work cannot duplicate consequences. Preview, Buy, Claim and Equip
must have distinct meanings.
Use `Tasks.collection-detail` for browsing and `Tasks.purchase` for accepted transactions.

## Shared decisions

Lobbies show participants, rules, readiness and what prevents Start. Voting
shows identifiable choices, the local selection, shared counts and a truthful
deadline. Confirm acceptance before presenting local intent as shared state.
Do not let an arriving prompt consume an unrelated preceding press.

Keep cancellation available where valid. Explain host departure, rejected
invitations and rule changes; retain recoverable drafts and accepted membership.

## Results and progression

Lead with personal outcome and accepted gains or losses. Standings support
that result. Show progress toward a meaningful next goal and a clear Continue,
Retry, Spectate or Leave action with its consequences.

Reward reveals may be skipped without losing or duplicating the grant. Keep
the accepted result visible after a transient celebration. Do not claim a
successful race, quest or job from a timer alone.

## Learning and interruption

Teach one useful action in context. Anchor guidance to the currently visible
form; guide through a compact menu before targeting an item inside it.
Keep the action usable, offer Skip and replay, and detect actual completion.
Use the player's current binding in instructions. Reveal guidance when the action
becomes relevant, then let the player practice it and see the result.
Keep help available again without forcing experienced players through the lesson.

Settings should remain reachable before inaccessible interaction becomes
mandatory. State whether the world continues during a menu. Preserve interrupted
drafts and restore useful focus and gameplay input on exit.

## Names and messages

Localize complete messages, including plurals and value order, rather than
joining translated fragments. Escape player or server text with richText.escape
before inserting it into rich formatting. Keep player text outside AutoLocalize.
Test long names, large numbers and unbroken text. Truncation must leave a route
to the full identity. Reserve room for captions and chat without hiding controls.

Use concrete action labels and state the consequence before commitment.
Explain an unavailable action at its source; a dim button alone gives no route forward.
For failures, say what remains unchanged and what the player can do next.
]]></string>
									<BinaryString name="AttributesSerialize"></BinaryString>
									<SecurityCapabilities name="Capabilities">0</SecurityCapabilities>
									<bool name="DefinesCapabilities">false</bool>
									<UniqueId name="HistoryId">00000000000000000000000000000000</UniqueId>
									<string name="Name">GAME_FLOWS_AND_INFORMATION.md</string>
									<BinaryString name="SerializedOverrides"></BinaryString>
									<int64 name="SourceAssetId">-1</int64>
									<SharedString name="Tags">yuZpQdnvvUBOTYh1jqZ2cA==</SharedString>
									<UniqueId name="UniqueId">1f5f3983a820f33d0aedc38900000793</UniqueId>
								</Properties>
							</Item>
							<Item class="StringValue" referent="952">
								<Properties>
									<string name="Value"><![CDATA[# Input, focus and access

Part of [game UI design](../GAME_UI_DESIGN_GUIDE.md).

Preserve the player's task across inputs. Entry, understanding, action,
correction and return must all work with the input the player uses. A shortcut
can make a route faster; it must not be the only way to discover a required
command. Make accessibility settings reachable before they are needed to
complete a difficult interaction.

## Separate the adaptation decisions

| Constraint | Evidence | Design response |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Available room | Container bounds, width and height classes, safe insets | Reflow groups, bound scrolling and retain the decision and its action |
| Physical readability | Display size, viewing distance, player text preference and device testing | Use readable type roles, contrast and an appropriate density |
| Available input | `interactionClasses` capabilities | Keep useful alternatives available |
| Current input | `effectiveInput` | Show relevant commands, glyphs and interaction feedback |
| Reach and precision | Held thumbs, pointer accuracy, focus traversal | Give targets space and offer discrete alternatives to gestures |

Use `UI.environment` for engine facts and device observation for physical
readability and reach. Read `environment.environment` and
`environment.viewingDistanceSource` for the exact sources and overrides.
A gamepad does not imply a television. A high-resolution handheld can have
many pixels but little physical reading space. A desktop player with a
gamepad still needs useful comparison detail. Choose from actual constraints,
not a device-name branch. Check a handheld such as a ROG Ally at its normal
holding distance; a resized desktop window cannot prove readability there.

Keep selection, drafts, scroll position and route identity when input changes.
A new glyph alone does not need a new screen. If a layout must change, keep
the selected object and next action recognizable. Read `effectiveInput`
instead of switching on every raw input event. Capabilities say what is
available; Roblox's `PreferredInput` estimates what the player is using.
Neither should disable another valid input.
[Roblox input reference](https://create.roblox.com/docs/reference/engine/classes/UserInputService#PreferredInput).

## Own input by task

Distinguish gameplay, UI navigation, text editing, manipulation and modal
response. A UI activation must not also jump, steer or fire. Use native
InputAction/InputContext for game commands and Facet's input mechanisms for
controls. Do not bind Roblox's Escape or gamepad Start.

A Composition is already passive, with `gameplayGuard = false` and
`engageOnPress = false`. Do not add a second responder. Give each HUD task an
authored gamepad command and a visible hint. Use `passive = false` for a
dedicated interface. For other HUD roots, use `UI.responder`; `engage` and
`resign` make the transition explicit. `engageOnPress = false` permits a press
to activate a control without taking the keyboard.

A page that replaces gameplay disables the game's gameplay InputContext and
custom touch actions. If it hides native movement controls, save and restore
the previous `GuiService.TouchControlsEnabled` value. Register restoration
with `Compose.cleanup` in the owning page, as in `Tasks.pages`. Close a child
surface without restoring gameplay while its parent still owns input. A
visible overlay does not pause the world; the game owns that decision.

Native InputContext priority and `Sink` resolve competing action bindings;
disabling the gameplay context makes its ownership explicit. The responder's
Space guard is not a general suspension of game commands.
[Roblox InputContext reference](https://create.roblox.com/docs/reference/engine/classes/InputContext).

## Make focus a useful route

Focus identifies the target of the next command. Keep it distinct from
persistent selection, equipped state and pending work. Show focus on the
actual actionable boundary. Let the control own its treatment; do not add a
second selection system. Check contrast over bright, busy and similar-colored
gameplay. Facet's default ring has a contrasting `chrome.focus.halo`; custom
bracket, glow and art treatments need equally visible boundaries.
[XAG 113: focus handling](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/xbox/accessibility/xbox-accessibility-guidelines/113).

Choose entry focus from the likely next action and the cost of an accidental
activation. When a Sheet takes selection, it uses `initialFocus`, then its
first available content control, then a pinned action, then Close or the
grabber. A Dialog prefers
Cancel, then a non-destructive action. A read-only result may need Close as
its useful action. Keep this judgment separate from the visual emphasis of
the primary action. Read `Selection.SelectedObject` for the exact behavior.

Make directional movement agree with the visible arrangement and keyboard
order agree with the reading and action order. Put frequent commands near
their related content; do not require a walk through a long collection to
reach them. Preserve logical item identity when rows recycle. Restore focus
to the opener on return, or to a useful remaining target when it disappears.
A HUD command that opens nothing hands the selection back to the game, so the
player presses it and keeps playing; Composition does this
(`resignOnActivate` in `responder`).
Back follows the actual task path. Show why an action is unavailable in
reachable text even when its disabled control is skipped.
[XAG 112: UI navigation](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/xbox/accessibility/xbox-accessibility-guidelines/112).

Focused content must remain visible, including inside nested scroll regions.
Use `scrollToVisible` for an event such as revealing an invalid field; do not
continuously recenter and fight the player's reading position. Use
`commandScope` for commands on native selected targets and `adjustable` for
specialized value controls. `commandScope` does not provide directional
navigation. A world-space target also needs a reachable command route; a
moving or occluded anchor is not a reliable place for sustained reading.

For world-space or VR work, read `Native-targets-and-boundaries` before choosing
interaction. Facet's world surfaces are flat UI; it supplies no ray, hand,
gaze input or VR layout mode. Plan and verify the required native input and
comfort behavior explicitly rather than assuming a screen layout proves it.

## Make commands discoverable

Use Facet's Roblox bindings consistently: ButtonA activates and ButtonB goes
back. TabView provides shoulder navigation. VirtualList, VirtualGrid, Table
and Outline provide trigger paging while they hold focus. The selected
control can own a more specific adjustment command; check its contract before
assigning the same button. Roblox keeps Menu; ButtonSelect enters native GUI
selection when `GuiService.AutoSelectGuiEnabled` is enabled.

Use `ShortcutHint` with an InputAction where available. It reads the native
preferred binding, including its display image or name. The hint describes
a binding; it does not create it. Use the engine's reported glyph instead of
assuming a controller brand. Keep the action name clear, such as "Equip"
rather than just "A".

A control can carry its own glyph with `cap = false`. Use `UI.ButtonGuide`
for contextual commands that have no visible control, such as paging or an
action on the focused item. List each command once. Prioritize useful commands
rather than filling a fixed quota. The guide is a noninteractive readout;
native selection and the surface's entry focus skip it.

Use an `action` entry for a native InputAction. It shows while the action's
native `Enabled` is on and the device has a binding. Use actions from
`commandScope`, or update their native `Enabled`, to follow the current focus.
Use `keyCode` for platform glyphs, including bumper and trigger pairs. Its
entry stays visible; update the entries when the command becomes unavailable.
`keys` uses written names that stay the same on every platform. A hint does
not create a command or its binding. Read `ButtonGuide.reference` for exact options.

The guide follows `effectiveInput` and shows for Gamepad by default. Add
`KeyboardAndMouse` to `inputs` when useful; Touch is not supported. Bind the
`buttonGuides` app or controls option to a readable player setting so players
can hide persistent hints. Keep the guide inside the owning surface's safe
area. In a Sheet, use `guide`: it stays outside every scroller and takes room
from the body while visible. Check short layouts with it shown. A guide hides
when a modal that does not contain it opens. Read `Sheet.guide` for placement.

Test device changes during a task, including controller disconnection. Keep
the draft and a usable exit; update hints without committing or restarting
the task. Keep valid pointer and controller inputs usable together; an event
from one must not lock out the other.
[Valve input guidance](https://partner.steamgames.com/doc/steamhardware/recommendations).

## Remove unnecessary access barriers

Provide a menu route with single digital presses. Do not require a pointer,
analog precision, a chord, rapid repeats or a sustained hold to configure and
use the interface. If a gesture improves the task, provide an equivalent
command. Make the alternative preserve the same eligibility, result and
recovery. Shortcuts supplement that route.
[XAG 107: input](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/xbox/accessibility/xbox-accessibility-guidelines/107).

Touch needs adequate target size, spacing and clearance from held thumbs and
native movement controls. Test the active hit area, not only the drawn icon.
Required information cannot depend on hover. Keep the active text field,
validation and completion or cancellation route reachable with the on-screen
keyboard open. Use native text editing and IME; keyboard dismissal must not
silently commit a consequential action.

Use semantic text roles and let larger text and localization reflow. Facet's
text follows `GuiService.PreferredTextSize`; do not use TextScaled to squeeze
it into a fixed box. Check the largest preference with real content. Keep
icons that convey text readable too. Facet's ten-foot theme and `overscanInsets`
provide a starting point, not proof of readability on every screen.
[Roblox text-size reference](https://create.roblox.com/docs/reference/engine/classes/GuiService#PreferredTextSize).

XAG 101 recommends a rendered text body height of at least 26 pixels at 1080p
for console use, with an option to double it. Body height includes ascenders,
the main letter body and descenders. These are viewing-context
guidelines, not universal Roblox TextSize values or a guarantee that Facet's
preference steps provide 200 percent scaling. Verify rendered text at the
intended distance and the actual range the game offers.
[XAG 101: text display](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/xbox/accessibility/xbox-accessibility-guidelines/101).

Name icon-only actions. Distinguish states with labels, shapes or patterns as
well as color. A read-only value can still need selection, copying and full
contrast. Combine visual, sound and haptic feedback where useful, with no
essential result available in only one sensory channel. Keep navigation
feedback quiet enough for repeated use. Reduced motion preserves the same
meaning and result without requiring a pulse or animation to understand it.

Verify the complete route: first HUD entry, action, correction, nested Back
and return to gameplay. Include removed targets, input changes and settings
changes while the task is open. Use Studio's controller emulation for native
behavior and physical devices for reach, readability and hardware claims.
]]></string>
									<BinaryString name="AttributesSerialize"></BinaryString>
									<SecurityCapabilities name="Capabilities">0</SecurityCapabilities>
									<bool name="DefinesCapabilities">false</bool>
									<UniqueId name="HistoryId">00000000000000000000000000000000</UniqueId>
									<string name="Name">INPUT_FOCUS_AND_ACCESS.md</string>
									<BinaryString name="SerializedOverrides"></BinaryString>
									<int64 name="SourceAssetId">-1</int64>
									<SharedString name="Tags">yuZpQdnvvUBOTYh1jqZ2cA==</SharedString>
									<UniqueId name="UniqueId">1f5f3983a820f33d0aedc38900000794</UniqueId>
								</Properties>
							</Item>
							<Item class="StringValue" referent="953">
								<Properties>
									<string name="Value"><![CDATA[# Layout and navigation

Part of [game UI design](../GAME_UI_DESIGN_GUIDE.md).

## Choose the relationship

Organize the information around what the player must compare and do. Keep
facts used in one decision together. Separate unrelated tasks before adding
panels. The same task can use a different arrangement when space changes,
but its identity, commitment and return route must remain clear.

Use VStack for reading order, HStack for one compact unit, Grid for a small
static arrangement, and Table for shared-attribute comparison. A results
page can contain a bounded Table inside a VStack; they solve different needs.

Screen arranges a vertical page with safe margins. It is not an overlay layer.
Mount Composition directly for a HUD. Ordinary interactive screens use device
chrome; edge-reaching artwork still needs safe text and controls.

## Fit without losing the task

Stacks hug by default. Bound the task before fill collections or wrapped text.
Use `width = "fill"` for the parent's width and `UI.fill()` for remaining
space beside siblings. Use `maxWidth` to keep a reading or decision area
comfortable in a wide viewport. Use a wrapping HStack (`wrap = true`) when
peer items can use several rows. Use AdaptiveStack when one group should
change between a row and a column. It keeps the same children mounted.

Allocate room to the required decision first. Let optional art, introductory
copy and secondary facts yield before the action and exit. Use ScrollView
for content that can extend beyond a bounded viewport. Keep a selected
object's commitment outside a long browsing collection. Do not let a preview
push that commitment below the shortest viewport.

For a small peer-card grid whose content sets the surface height, use
VirtualGrid with `minColumnWidth`, `mode = "all"` and `hug = true`.
Its container sets the columns and shared row width. Keep a large catalog
in a bounded scrolling collection instead. Read `VirtualList-and-VirtualGrid.sizing`
and `VirtualList-and-VirtualGrid.mode` for the exact sizing contract.

Adapt to the containing surface, including its height. `UI.environment(source)`
can read a native container's room; a camera viewport is not the width of a
nested panel. Prefer native layout and controls that measure their own room.
Input and viewing distance are separate: a near gamepad player can still use
two readable panes. See [input](INPUT_FOCUS_AND_ACCESS.md) for those decisions.

Composition ranks competing Regions; lower ranks take precedence. Each form
needs a measurable size and a useful complete meaning. A fill-sized form cannot
describe its intrinsic requirement. Use mayDrop only when an immediate required
action has an equally usable alternative. A compact Menu owns its own action
and must actually open the omitted choices.

ViewThatFits tries alternatives in order and still shows the last if none fit.
Its final form must work at the smallest supported room. It does not decide
which information is safe to remove. Give candidates an intrinsic or fixed
size; a fill-width candidate always passes the width check. Candidates are
separate mounted trees, so shared values belong in the model. Create a new
Instance for each candidate rather than parenting the same Instance twice.

## Navigate deliberately

TabView is for stable peer destinations. NavigationStack is for a meaningful
path into detail. NavigationSplitView is for a collection whose selection
determines related detail, not two unrelated panels.

Keep the title, selected section and Back destination clear. Preserve selection,
drafts and useful scroll position on return. In compact split views, deliberate
activation opens detail and Back returns to the collection. A directional
focus move should not unexpectedly replace the collection. Set pane minimums
from readable content rather than forcing two unusable panes. Keep actions in
a stable order across arrangements so players can use what they learned.

NavigationSplitView retains both panes when it changes arrangement. Bind its
`preferredCompactColumn` and set it to `"detail"` when an activation opens the
selected object. Back writes `"sidebar"`. `minSidebarWidth`, `minDetailWidth`
and `compactWidth` express the room needed by the content; `axis = "y"` has
equivalent height options. Use the installed contract for those options.

NavigationStack's writable `path` owns the route. TabView's writable
`selection` owns the peer destination. Both use Compose LayerStack for page
lifetime. Keep durable values outside pages even when visited pages are
retained; TabView can dispose departing pages with `retention = "top"`.
Use Compose branches for changing content inside a page, not a second route
history or an extra application mount.

NavBar arranges navigation chrome but does not own history. Hiding built-in
chrome requires a complete visible return route. A root page has no Back.
Do not turn every small setting adjustment into another page.

When a destination contains compact split navigation, use Close to return to
gameplay and label the inner Back with its collection name. Two controls named
Back must not lead to different places. Test the composed page and body together;
their headers and safe margins consume the same limited height.

PageView suits a short inspectable sequence; expose an alternative to swiping.
Pagination suits genuinely bounded result pages. StepIndicator describes task
stages, not destinations. ZoomView preserves spatial relationships in a map or
board; keep global actions outside its zoomed content.

## Check the connected route

Open the task from gameplay, enter detail, change the viewport while it is
open, return and reopen. Check the shortest room with long labels and the
on-screen keyboard. Confirm that the task keeps its state and that focus,
scroll position and gameplay input return to useful places.

Read `Tasks.pages` for the destination and input lifetime, then
`Tasks.collection-detail` or `Tasks.tabs` for its body. Read
`Layout-options`, `fill`, `AdaptiveStack.reference`, `ViewThatFits.reference`
and the chosen navigation control's contract for exact configuration.
]]></string>
									<BinaryString name="AttributesSerialize"></BinaryString>
									<SecurityCapabilities name="Capabilities">0</SecurityCapabilities>
									<bool name="DefinesCapabilities">false</bool>
									<UniqueId name="HistoryId">00000000000000000000000000000000</UniqueId>
									<string name="Name">LAYOUT_AND_NAVIGATION.md</string>
									<BinaryString name="SerializedOverrides"></BinaryString>
									<int64 name="SourceAssetId">-1</int64>
									<SharedString name="Tags">yuZpQdnvvUBOTYh1jqZ2cA==</SharedString>
									<UniqueId name="UniqueId">1f5f3983a820f33d0aedc38900000795</UniqueId>
								</Properties>
							</Item>
							<Item class="StringValue" referent="954">
								<Properties>
									<string name="Value"><![CDATA[# Motion and feedback

Part of [game UI design](../GAME_UI_DESIGN_GUIDE.md).

Motion explains a change: where a page went, what gained focus, which value
changed, or where an accepted object arrived. It must not delay essential input
or make the player follow an animation to learn the result.

Separate immediate input feedback from progress and accepted outcome.
A pressed state can confirm that input arrived while the game still awaits a server result.
Keep the final result readable after motion ends. Reinforce urgent feedback with
more than one signal; sound, haptics or color alone can be missed.

Use theme timing and easing. Controls already provide shared feedback; do not
stack a custom loop on the same property. Use native StyleSheet transitions
where supported and `runtime.tween` or `runtime.spring` for other reactive motion.
Do not create another animation scheduler.

Use `Custom-transitions` for Sheet, TabView and NavigationStack presentation.
Bind through the supplied progress and owner so interruption and cleanup remain coherent.
For other Compose motion, supply a reactive `reducedMotion` policy; Compose core
does not discover the platform preference. Read `Motion.motionLevel` for Facet's policy.

Keep direct manipulation attached to input. After release, motion can explain
arrival or return. Hover and press must not change measured layout, move hit
targets or clip the emphasized control. Keep text readable during transitions.

Use Travel for an object moving between meaningful locations and `UI.sequence`
for ordered presentation. Keep accepted ownership in the model, independent
of either animation. Skip, interruption, navigation away and reduced motion
must preserve the same final state and cleanup.
Read `Effect-sequences` and `Travel.reference` for their contracts.

Reduced motion can execute sequence callbacks immediately. Do not depend on
a duration to grant rewards, release input or complete a task. Repeated state
updates should retarget or settle coherently rather than accumulating effects.

Test rapid reversal, repeat activation, resizing, theme changes, removal,
navigation during motion and reduced motion. At a fixed viewport, an idle
surface must settle. Measure frame cost and retained resources across repeated
navigation, not only during one quiet frame.
]]></string>
									<BinaryString name="AttributesSerialize"></BinaryString>
									<SecurityCapabilities name="Capabilities">0</SecurityCapabilities>
									<bool name="DefinesCapabilities">false</bool>
									<UniqueId name="HistoryId">00000000000000000000000000000000</UniqueId>
									<string name="Name">MOTION_AND_FEEDBACK.md</string>
									<BinaryString name="SerializedOverrides"></BinaryString>
									<int64 name="SourceAssetId">-1</int64>
									<SharedString name="Tags">yuZpQdnvvUBOTYh1jqZ2cA==</SharedString>
									<UniqueId name="UniqueId">1f5f3983a820f33d0aedc38900000796</UniqueId>
								</Properties>
							</Item>
							<Item class="StringValue" referent="955">
								<Properties>
									<string name="Value"><![CDATA[# Surfaces and feedback

Part of [game UI design](../GAME_UI_DESIGN_GUIDE.md).

## Decide what interrupts

Use the least interruption that makes the decision clear. Consider urgency,
consequence and whether the player must keep watching or controlling the world.
Keep information at its source when it helps the current task. Take attention
only when proceeding without a decision would create a meaningful problem.

DisclosureGroup expands optional facts in the reading flow. Popover attaches
a quick task to its source. Callout teaches a contextual action. Alert confirms
a brief consequential decision. Dialog or Sheet owns a bounded interruption.
Recurring destinations generally need pages.

Separate required facts from optional detail. A price, risk or reason an action
is unavailable must not depend on hover. Progressive disclosure should reduce
distraction while keeping the next step and its consequence discoverable.
For example, an optional stat breakdown can expand beside an upgrade, while
its cost and effect stay beside the commitment.

A modal does not automatically pause the game or suppress custom gameplay
commands. Define input ownership, visible exits and recovery. Do not stack
modals to simulate navigation.

Popover's default `compact = "sheet"` adapts an anchored task for narrow touch
room. Use the control's route change and keep durable state in the model.
The same task needs a clear title, commitment and exit in either presentation.

For a floating Sheet, center placement has a visible Close and no grabber.
Edge docking serves tasks where resizing or edge dismissal helps. Leave
closeButton absent to let the exit follow placement. Use hug for short intrinsic
content; bound and scroll collections. Keep actions reachable at every detent.
Apply Sheet.dismissal to all dirty exits. Pinned actions do not close it
automatically; rejected work must remain usable.

Alert dismisses before invoking its action. Use a controlled Dialog or Sheet
when the task must remain open for an accepted or rejected request. Read the
chosen contract before wiring dismissal or pending behavior.

Dialog reads `isPresented` and requests changes through `onPresentedChange`;
the model accepts or refuses the request. Its actions propose closure by
default; `keepOpen = true` suppresses that proposal. Sheet actions never close
the sheet. Use `Sheet.dismissal` for its guard instead of copying Dialog's
action options. Keep one dismissal policy for Close, Back, outside presses,
gestures and actions that leave a dirty task.

## Explain state where it matters

Use Notice for persistent state, Toast for a brief acknowledgement, Badge or
StatusIndicator for a compact attached signal. Keep required consequences
available after a toast expires. Aggregate repeated gains and remove messages
whose context no longer applies.
Do not dismiss the only explanation for a blocked action while it remains
blocked. An expiring Undo must not be the only way to prevent serious loss.

Put feedback near the object or action that changed. State what happened and
what the player can do next. Match emphasis to urgency; repeated routine
success should not compete with danger or the player's current decision.
Use a confirmation only when it helps the player understand or prevent a
meaningful error. Name the object and consequence in its action labels.

ProgressView shows a meaningful quantity or unknown wait. Timer displays a
deadline; zero does not independently authorize rewards or completion.
Skeleton reserves known loading structure. Keep loading separate from an
empty result. Show progress only from a known measure; use an unknown wait
when the game cannot measure completion. Retrying must not create a second
purchase or repeat an already accepted action.

ErrorBoundary contains failures while building or updating its UI region.
Its `fallback(failure, retry)` rebuilds that region when retried. A rejected
server request is model state, not a rendering failure. Show its explanation
and recovery through normal bound content, using Compose.show or Compose.switch
when the structure changes. Keep useful input available in either case.

## Give content an intentional frame

Use semantic Text roles, readable contrast and bounded wrapping. Text and
important actions must survive missing art, long labels and larger text.
ShortcutHint describes an actual binding; it does not create one.

Image and AsyncImage need stable dimensions and a useful failure state.
Stage should frame the object clearly at a useful scale; inspection controls
must not also activate the surrounding item. AvatarGroup summarizes membership;
use a list when players must compare individual status.
Use a static image when it conveys the same decision at lower cost. Create
expensive previews only when useful; keep stable space while they load.

Typewriter needs a finish action distinct from advancing dialogue. Reduced
motion and Skip still reveal the complete result. Keep accepted state
independent of presentation timing.

## Verify interruption and return

Open from a meaningful source, act, cancel, reject a request and reopen. Check
that feedback describes the accepted state, the previous task remains usable,
and focus returns to the source or a useful surviving target. Change room and
input while the surface is open. Test the visible exit and Back in every
presentation, including a short viewport with the keyboard open.

Read `Tasks.sheet`, `Sheet.layout`, `Sheet.dismissal`, `Dialog.Dialog`,
`Alert.reference` and `Popover.compact` for presentation ownership. Read
`ErrorBoundary.reference` and `Typewriter.reference` for their recovery and
completion contracts.
]]></string>
